VERDICT
The code is logically correct because it successfully finds powers of two by generating them one by one and stopping early, though it relies on slow decimal math functions instead of integer operations.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You handled negative numbers naturally because the loop breaks or does not run. You used a long long to prevent numbers from growing too large and crashing. You broke out of the loop early as soon as the calculated power became larger than the target number.

Is it correct? Yes, it passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: The loop condition i <= n/2 is confusing. For n = 16, n/2 is 8, but the answer is 2 to the power of 4, so i only goes up to 4. The loop stops early because of your exp > n check, so n/2 is never reached. Using the pow function is also a bad habit here because it is meant for decimal numbers, not exact integers, and it takes more time for the computer to process.

Time and space complexity of MY code: Time complexity is O(log n) because the loop only runs up to the power of the number (around 31 times maximum). Space complexity is O(1) because you only use a couple of simple variables.

Why my approach works: You are just generating 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 and checking if any of them perfectly match your target number. If the generated number gets bigger than your target, you know it is not a power of two and you stop.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Loop and Generate (Your Approach, improved)

Idea: Generate powers of 2 using simple multiplication and compare them to the number until you find a match or go over.

Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
        long long exp = 1;
        while (exp <= n) {
            if (exp == n) return true;
            exp = exp * 2;
        }
        return false;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(log n), Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: We can test the number itself by dividing it, instead of building up new numbers.

Approach 2: Keep Dividing by 2

Idea: If a number is a power of 2, you can divide it by 2 evenly all the way down to 1. If it ever has a remainder when dividing by 2, it is not a power of 2.

Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        while (n % 2 == 0) {
            n = n / 2;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(log n), Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: Computers can look at the binary code (the 1s and 0s) of the number to answer the question instantly without any loops.

Approach 3: The Bitwise Trick

Idea: In binary code, a power of two is a 1 followed by all 0s (like 1000 for the number 8). Subtracting 1 flips all those bits (0111 for the number 7). If you use the bitwise AND operator (&) on them, it compares them and leaves 0.

Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        return (n & (n - 1)) == 0;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(1) because it is a single math step, Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: This is the best approach, but you can also use a built-in counting tool.

Approach 4: Count the 1s

Idea: Since a power of two has exactly one 1 in its binary code, we can just count the 1s using a built-in CPU tool.

Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        return __builtin_popcount(n) == 1;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(1), Space is O(1).

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 1, building up powers to check against the target.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? An interviewer absolutely expects Approach 3 (The Bitwise Trick). This specific question is famous for testing if you know that one exact trick.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question: Can you solve it without writing a loop?

Answer: Yes, by using the bitwise AND trick with n and n-1.

Question: Why do you need to check if n is greater than 0 first?

Answer: Because negative numbers and zero cannot be powers of two, but the bitwise trick alone would mistakenly say zero is a power of two.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER

The key insight, in one sentence: A power of two only has one bit set to 1 in binary, and the trick n & (n - 1) clears that lowest 1 bit.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Do not use decimal math functions like pow() for whole numbers.

Make sure your loop conditions make sense, instead of relying only on an internal break to stop it.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: Bit manipulation and understanding how numbers look in binary.

